From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jon Pretty <jon.pretty@propensive.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 18:25:24 +0900
Subject: Compile a unit read from TASTy in the source its trees were written
 in

The source of a unit read from TASTy is its TASTy file, while its trees are
given the source file they were written in, whose path and line lengths TASTy
records. Three things went by the unit's source where the trees' was meant.

The backend named the TASTy file in the `SourceFile` attribute of every class
it emitted for the unit, so a stack trace read `Trace.tasty`.

When an `Inlined` node is dropped, code inlined from another file is moved to
the position of the call, since a class file has one source, and code from the
unit's own file keeps its position. Every tree of the unit was in another
file than the unit's, so all inlined code was moved, and placed in the TASTy
file, where its position fell on line 1.

And each phase made its new trees in the context's source, which is the
unit's, so synthesized code had no line at all.

A unit now has a `treeSource`, the source of its tree where the unit was read
from TASTy and its own source otherwise, which is what the backend names, what
the inliner compares against and moves code to, and what a context for the
unit has as its source. The unit's source is unchanged, and with it the files
of the run and what counts as defined in it.

Written with LLM assistance (Claude Code); reviewed content.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Proscala-Patch: tastysource
